Object.assign(EXTRAS,{
 p3l9:{plain:{version:"Addiction is a habit rooted in the body and brain that makes it very hard, but not impossible, to stop. Responsibility is real but often reduced. Twelve-step programmes, motivational interviewing, CBT and medication all help, and grace and community are central to recovery.",question:"Is addiction a disease or a sin?",one:"A bodily-rooted bad habit that diminishes freedom; treat it, take responsibility, seek grace."},
  hook:"\"My grace is sufficient for thee\" (2 Cor 12:9).",
  analogy:{title:"The path through long grass",text:"Walk the same way through long grass many times and a path forms; soon your feet take it without thinking. Addiction is a deep path. Recovery means walking a new one, often with company, until it too becomes easy."},
  sort:{title:"Which DSM-5-TR criterion group?",cats:["Impaired control","Social impairment","Risky use","Pharmacological"],items:[["Craving",0],["Missing work due to use",1],["Drinking and driving",2],["Tolerance",3],["Failed attempts to cut down",0],["Withdrawal symptoms",3]]}},
 p3l10:{plain:{version:"Psychosis is a serious break in contact with reality, with delusions, voices or disorganised thinking. For Thomas, the soul is untouched but the bodily powers it uses are disturbed. Antipsychotics, family support and recovery-oriented care help; spiritual explanations must not delay treatment.",question:"Is psychosis a spiritual attack?",one:"Usually an illness of the brain affecting the mind's instruments; treat it, and offer spiritual support."},
  hook:"The Gerasene, \"clothed, and well in his wits\" (Mark 5:15).",
  analogy:{title:"A scratched lens",text:"A skilled photographer with a scratched lens takes distorted pictures. The photographer is unchanged; the lens needs repair. In psychosis, the intellect is intact, but its instrument, the imagination, is damaged."},
  sort:{title:"Positive or negative symptom?",cats:["Positive","Negative"],items:[["Hearing voices",0],["Reduced motivation",1],["Delusions of persecution",0],["Flat emotional expression",1],["Disorganised speech",0],["Social withdrawal",1]]}},
 p3l11:{plain:{version:"Grief is love's response to loss. It comes in waves and usually integrates over time. About one in ten bereaved people develop prolonged grief and need help. Jesus wept; Christians grieve, but with hope, and continue to love the dead through prayer.",question:"How long should grief last, and is it a lack of faith?",one:"There is no timetable; grief is not faithlessness; seek help if it stays stuck after 6–12 months."},
  hook:"\"Jesus wept\" (John 11:35).",
  analogy:{title:"The ball in the box",text:"Grief is like a ball in a box with a pain button. At first the ball is huge and constantly hits the button. Over time the ball doesn't shrink so much as the box grows: it hits the button less often, but when it does, it still hurts."},
  sort:{title:"Normal grief or prolonged grief disorder?",cats:["Normal grief","Prolonged grief disorder"],items:[["Waves of sadness months later, but life resuming",0],["Two years on, cannot accept the death and life feels meaningless",1],["Sensing the person's presence briefly",0],["Avoiding all reminders, unable to function",1],["Crying at the anniversary",0]]}},
 p3l12:{plain:{version:"Suicide is preventable but hard to predict. Ask directly, listen, make a safety plan together, restrict access to means, and connect the person with help. The Church teaches that suicide is gravely wrong, but that mental illness can greatly reduce responsibility, and she prays for those who have died and does not despair of their salvation.",question:"What do I do if someone is suicidal, and what does the Church say?",one:"Ask, stay, plan for safety, connect to help; and trust God's mercy for those who have died."},
  hook:"\"Do thyself no harm, for we all are here\" (Acts 16:28).",
  analogy:{title:"The storm and the anchor",text:"A suicidal crisis is like a storm: intense, but it passes. A safety plan is an anchor that holds the boat until the storm blows over."},
  sort:{title:"Risk factor or protective factor?",cats:["Risk factor","Protective factor"],items:[["Previous attempt",0],["Strong family connectedness",1],["Access to lethal means",0],["Engagement in treatment",1],["Hopelessness",0],["Religious community participation",1]]}}
});
